  "slug": "nitrogen-doped-94d4",
  "summary": "Recent preprints apply optical spectroscopy and electrostatic modeling to quantify non-uniform internal fields and doping in AlGaN, MoSe2 and diamond devices.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how doping and interfaces set internal electric fields and charge distributions in semiconductor and defect-hosting devices. Exciton Franz-Keldysh photocurrent spectroscopy on AlGaN p-i-n photodiodes extracts local voltages and reveals non-uniform internal electric fields. Poisson-Fermi calculations for boron-doped/nitrogen-doped diamond junctions indicate that interface donor ionization governs NV charge-state depletion depth more than bulk boron. A third preprint characterizes monolayer MoSe2 encapsulated in amorphous silicon nitride against an hBN reference, reporting electron doping and Fermi-polaron resonances. The new elements are the use of excitonic spectroscopy as a local field probe and the identification of interface, rather than bulk, donor ionization as the controlling factor in the diamond case. The MoSe2 study addresses encapsulation-induced doping rather than field mapping.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-18"
}
